Dictionary in Python
Q1. Student Database

Aim:
To create a menu-driven Student Database using nested dictionaries.
The program allows adding, updating, deleting and displaying student records.
It also generates reports such as average marks, topper, subject-wise highest scorers and grade-wise grouping.

Sample Input:
101: Asha, {Math: 88, Physics: 76, Chem: 92}
102: Ravi, {Math: 35, Physics: 58, Chem: 64}
103: Meena, {Math: 72, Physics: 81, Chem: 69}

Sample Output:
Averages:
101 -> 85.33
102 -> 52.33
103 -> 74.00

Topper: Asha

Highest per subject:
Math -> Asha (88)
Physics -> Meena (81)
Chem -> Asha (92)

Below 40 in any subject:
Ravi (Math 35)

Adding roll no. 101 again:
Rejected as duplicate.

Marks of 105:
Rejected as out of range

Q2. Employee Database (Grouping and Aggregation)

Aim:
To create an Employee Database using dictionaries to store employee ID, name, department and salary.
The program performs operations such as adding, updating, deleting and searching employees, along with department-wise payroll and salary reports.

Sample Input:
E1: Kiran, IT, 60000
E2: Sara, HR, 45000
E3: Dev, IT, 75000
E4: Nina, Finance, 52000
E5: Omar, HR, 48000

Sample Output:
Department-wise Payroll:
IT = 135000
HR = 93000
Finance = 52000

Highest Paid Employee:
IT -> Dev
HR -> Omar
Finance -> Nina

After 10% Raise:
Sara = 49500
Omar = 52800

Original Salaries:
Sara = 45000
Omar = 48000

Department with Highest Average Salary:
IT = 67500

Deleting E9:
Employee not found.

Q3. Library Record System (State and Rules)

Aim:
To develop a Library Record System using dictionaries to maintain book details, available copies and issued member IDs.
The program performs book issuing, returning and searching while applying rules such as maximum two books per member and checking book availability.

Sample Input:
B1: Python Basics, Rao, 2 copies
B2: Data Structures, Iyer, 1 copy
B3: Algorithms, Rao, 1 copy

Sample Output:
Issue B1 to M1:
Success, copies = 1

Issue B1 to M2:
Success, copies = 0

Issue B1 to M3:
Rejected, no copies left.

Issue B1 to M1 again:
Rejected, already holds it.

Return B1 by M9:
Rejected, M9 does not hold it.

Return B1 by M1:
Success, copies = 1

Issue B2 to M2:
Success.

Issue B3 to M2:
Rejected, member has reached the 2-book limit.

Q4. Word Frequency Counter (Dictionary as a Counter)

Aim:
To develop a Word Frequency Counter using dictionaries to count the occurrences of words in a paragraph. 
The program cleans text, removes punctuation, displays the top N frequent words and groups words according to their frequency and length.

Sample Input 1:
Enter paragraph:
the cat and the hat and the bat

Enter N:
3

Sample Output 1:
Word Counts:
the = 3
and = 2
bat = 1
cat = 1
hat = 1

Top 3:
the (3)
and (2)
bat (1)

Inverted Dictionary:
{3: ['the'], 2: ['and'], 1: ['bat', 'cat', 'hat']}

Words Appearing Once:
bat, cat, hat

Sample Input 2:
Hello, hello! HELLO world.

Sample Output 2:
hello = 3
world = 1

Sample Input 3:
Empty string

Sample Output 3:
No words were entered. Nothing to count.

Q5. Phone Directory (List Values and Reverse Lookup)

Aim:
To create a Phone Directory using dictionaries and lists to store contact names, phone numbers, email addresses and cities.
The program supports adding, updating, deleting and searching contacts, along with reverse phone lookup and city-wise grouping.

Sample Input:
Anita: [9876543210], anita@mail.com, Pune
Manoj: [9123456780, 9988776655],
       manoj@mail.com, Delhi
Zara: [9000011111], zara@mail.com, Pune

Sample Output:
Search "an":
Anita
Manoj

Reverse Lookup 9988776655:
Manoj

Contacts Grouped
                            -------------------------------------
